        "id": "69ba67ca",
        "verdict": "reasoning",
        "was": "B",
        "explanation": "The User-Agent flow (implicit grant) with a refresh token (B) is the traditional exam answer for 'after first login, never re-prompt' on mobile. With 'refresh_token' scope, the refresh token is stored on the device and silently renews access tokens — so the requirement is met.\nHowever, Salesforce now explicitly recommends the Web Server flow with PKCE for native and mobile apps instead of the User-Agent flow. The User-Agent / implicit grant delivers the access token in the URL fragment, creating a token-leakage risk through browser history and redirect-URL interception. Salesforce allows administrators to disable the User-Agent flow entirely at the Connected App level for this reason.\nThe Web Server flow with a refresh token (option D) satisfies the same 'no re-login' requirement while the authorization code exchange is done server-side with a PKCE code verifier, eliminating the fragment-exposure risk. On the current Salesforce platform, D is the architecturally preferred choice for new mobile implementations.\nReferences:\nhttps://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=xcloud.remoteaccess_oauth_user_agent_flow.htm&language=en_US&type=5\nhttps://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=sf.remoteaccess_oauth_web_server_flow.htm&language=en_US&type=5",
        "note": "Answer key B stands (User-Agent is the legacy exam answer). Explanation updated to note that Salesforce's current guidance favors the Web Server flow with PKCE for mobile (option D), so learners understand the modern recommendation alongside the exam key.",
