        "id": "7fbbab5d",
        "verdict": "reasoning",
        "was": "A",
        "explanation": "JIT (Just-in-Time) provisioning creates or updates a Salesforce user record when the user successfully authenticates via SAML — so it handles provisioning at first login. JIT cannot deprovision users because deprovisioning means the user can no longer log in at all, which means the JIT handler never fires. An explicit API call from the IdP or an admin tool is required to deactivate the user in Salesforce.\nThe answer therefore requires the IdP product to support (A) SAML JIT for provisioning plus an API (SOAP or SCIM) for deprovisioning. SCIM is now the modern standard for deprovisioning, but the answer option uses 'SOAP API' as the deprovisioning mechanism — both SOAP and SCIM are valid, but SOAP API is what the option set provides.\nOptions B and C (JIT for both, or API for both) misunderstand the fundamental limitation: JIT is event-driven by authentication, so it cannot independently initiate a deprovisioning action.\nReferences:\nhttps://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=sf.sso_jit_about.htm&language=en_US&type=5\nhttps://developer.salesforce.com/docs/atlas.en-us.api.meta/api/sforce_api_calls_create.htm",
