        "explanation": "The User-Agent (implicit grant) flow uses three core OAuth concepts: (D) Client ID — identifies the Connected App in the initial redirect; (C) Scopes — declare what resources the token can access; and (E) Refresh Token — returned alongside the access token when the 'refresh_token' scope is included in the request.\nThe critical distinction from the Web Server flow is the absence of (B) an Authorization Code — the implicit grant returns the access token directly in the URL fragment without a server-to-server exchange step. (A) Verification Code belongs to the Device Authentication flow, not this one.\nA subtlety on refresh tokens: the strict OAuth 2.0 specification does not allow refresh tokens in the implicit grant. Salesforce's implementation extends this by returning a refresh token when the 'refresh_token' scope is explicitly requested — which is what makes option E valid in the Salesforce context.\nReferences:\nhttps://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=xcloud.remoteaccess_oauth_user_agent_flow.htm&language=en_US&type=5",
