        "verdict": "reasoning",
        "was": "A,B,E",
        "explanation": "Three items to consider when building a Delegated Authentication web service:\n(A) Whitelist Salesforce IP ranges on the corporate firewall — Salesforce makes an outbound SOAP call to the Delegated Auth endpoint on every login, so the corporate firewall must permit traffic from Salesforce's published egress IP ranges.\n(B) Include sourceIp as a method parameter — the Delegated Auth WSDL defines exactly three input parameters: username, password, and sourceIp. The sourceIp can be used inside the service for additional IP-based security decisions.\n(E) Return a Boolean — the SOAP method must return true (valid credentials) or false (invalid). Salesforce uses this value directly to approve or reject the login.\n(C) is false: Delegated Authentication is SOAP-only. The Salesforce-published WSDL defines the entire contract, and a REST endpoint cannot fulfill it.\n(D) is a dangerous distractor: enabling Delegated Auth on the System Administrator profile means that if the endpoint goes down, no administrator can log in to diagnose the outage. Best practice is to keep at least one System Administrator on standard Salesforce authentication as a recovery fallback.\nReferences:\nhttps://help.salesforce.com/s/articleView?id=sf.sso_delauthentication.htm&language=en_US&type=5",
